=[ Telaion: architecture ]

Status: draft. Written before the first component. Items marked "Open" are decisions that have not been made.

==[ Purpose ]

Two Tomet editors exist today and each was written on its own: the inline editor of `tomet-wiki` (a browser bundle embedded in a Rust crate) and `ordiola` (a Tauri desktop app). Both do the same job: load a `.tmt` source, let a person edit it, save it, and show conflicts. Rewriting that for every editor, and rewriting its look each time, is the cost this repository removes.

Telaion is the shared frame. It carries structure and behavior. It carries no theme beyond a minimal default.

==[ Name ]

`telaio` is Italian for a loom or a frame. Telaion derives from it. Its first application is `ordiola`, named after the warp (`ordito`) that is strung first on a loom so that the weft can be passed through it.

==[ Packages ]

-| `@telaion/ui` -- Generic primitives (button, input, modal, tabs, tree, split) and the design tokens. No Tomet knowledge.
-| `@telaion/editor` -- The editor frame. Takes a storage adapter and a language slot. No Tomet knowledge.
-| `@telaion/tomet` -- Tomet-specific parts: syntax highlighting, conflict blocks and their resolution. It knows the shapes of Tomet data, not the engine that computes them.

The split is by what a package must know, not by size. `@telaion/ui` can be extracted into its own repository later without touching the others.

==[ Dependency direction ]

```text
tomet-wiki, ordiola     each owns one Tomet engine (@tomet/cms, @tomet/wasm, or Rust)
    |   supplies TometServices and a DocumentAdapter
    v
@telaion/tomet          knows the shapes of Tomet data; no engine
    ^
@telaion/editor         knows nothing about Tomet
    ^
@telaion/ui             knows nothing about Tomet or the editor
```

Telaion points up at nothing: it imports no Tomet package. The arrows into it are the interfaces an application fills in.

==[ No Tomet engine inside ]

The wiki already ships `@tomet/cms` as WebAssembly, and `ordiola` runs Tomet as Rust behind its desktop shell. If Telaion carried its own copy of Tomet, each application would hold two. So Telaion carries none. Whatever it needs from Tomet comes in through a small interface, in the same way a storage adapter brings in storage.

```ts
interface TometServices {
  /** Ranges to color. Offsets are UTF-16 code units, as CodeMirror counts them. */
  highlight(source: string): HighlightSpan[] | Promise<HighlightSpan[]>;
  listConflicts(source: string): Conflict[] | Promise<Conflict[]>;
  resolveConflict(source: string, index: number, side: "a" | "b"): string | Promise<string>;
}
```

-| Every method may be asynchronous. A WebAssembly engine answers at once; a desktop shell reaches Rust through a message and answers later. Telaion handles a late answer for an old text by ignoring it.
-| The types are defined in `@telaion/tomet`. An application writes a few lines that turn the output of its engine into them, for example converting byte offsets to UTF-16 when the engine counts bytes.
-| Where the logic lives: in the Tomet crates, once. The engines that applications use are different packaging of the same code.

===[ Highlighting ]

`tometHighlighting(services)` in `@telaion/tomet` is a CodeMirror extension; it is passed as the `language` of a `CodeEditor`.

-| It asks `services.highlight` after every edit and paints the ranges it gets. The ranges are kept in the editor state, so they follow the text while the next answer is on its way.
-| An answer for text that has since changed is dropped. A slow or message-based engine never paints old ranges over new text.
-| When the engine fails, the last good colors stay, the editor keeps working, and `onError` is told. Every edit asks again.
-| The colors are the design tokens: `heading`, `tagName` and `list` in the accent; `string`, `number` and `atom` in the ok color; comments, punctuation and separators muted; `monospace` on the sunken surface. A tag that is not listed stays in the plain text color.
-| `utf8ToUtf16Spans` turns spans counted in bytes into spans counted in UTF-16, for an engine that counts bytes.

==[ Why Svelte ]

Asked before the first component, while a change was still free. The answer is to stay on Svelte 5. React is excluded on its own merits, not only on familiarity.

-| The heart of an editor is not the framework. CodeMirror and ProseMirror manage their own DOM; the framework only draws the frame around them (modal, tabs, toolbar, conflict view). What matters is how cleanly an imperative DOM library can be embedded.
-| Svelte compiles away. It has no virtual DOM, so `mount()` places a component into a node and does not fight an editor over that node's DOM. A virtual-DOM framework needs wrappers and portals for the same job.
-| The wiki embeds an editing island into a statically generated page. Mounting a component into a given element on demand is what Svelte does best.
-| Scoped component CSS plus the tokens of `@telaion/ui` is enough. No CSS-in-JS and no utility framework are needed, which is what the token rule requires.

Considered and not chosen: Solid (equivalent weight and reactivity, no gain, a thinner ecosystem), Lit and Web Components (shadow DOM makes editor styling harder), Leptos and Dioxus (the editing core stays in JavaScript, so the JavaScript remains).

===[ The public boundary stays framework-neutral ]

The plug-in API of an application, and any other boundary that third parties write against, is expressed in DOM and TypeScript: register a command, hand a panel an element to fill. It does not take or return Svelte components. A plug-in author must not have to match a Svelte version. The application's own panels can still be Svelte, mounted into the same elements.

==[ Out of scope ]

The following stay in the applications.
-| The application shell: file tree, workspace and vault management, tabs, settings.
-| Authentication and anything that talks to a host (Tauri `invoke`, the GitHub API).
-| Rust code. Metadata handling that needs the Tomet AST belongs to the Tomet crates, not to Telaion.

==[ The storage adapter ]

Components never know where a document lives. The application passes an adapter; a desktop app backs it with local files, the wiki backs it with the GitHub API.

This is a sketch of the shape, not a final API.

```ts
export interface DocumentAdapter {
  load(path: string): Promise<{ content: string; rev?: string }>;
  save(path: string, content: string, base?: string): Promise<SaveResult>;
}

export type SaveResult =
  | { ok: true; rev?: string }
  | { ok: false; conflict: { remote: string; rev?: string } };
```

==[ The editor frame ]

`@telaion/editor` has three layers, each usable alone.

-| `DocumentSession` -- one document being edited: what was loaded, what it is now (`dirty`), the status, the last error, and a conflict when a save found a newer stored version. It has no view. It is written with Svelte runes (`.svelte.ts`).
-| `CodeEditor` -- the text area, on CodeMirror 6. It takes `content` as a bindable value and reports edits and save requests. The language is a slot (a CodeMirror extension): the editor itself does not know Tomet.
-| `Editor` -- a session and a `CodeEditor` with a path, a status badge, a Save button and the alerts for errors and conflicts.

```ts
type CodeEditorProps = {
  content: string; // $bindable
  readOnly?: boolean;
  label?: string; // accessible name of the text area
  language?: Extension; // supplied by @telaion/tomet
  onChange?: (content: string) => void; // edits by a person, not replacements from outside
  onSave?: () => void; // Ctrl or Cmd + S
};
```

===[ Decided ]

-| The engine is CodeMirror 6 and nothing else: one way to edit, one entry point. A minimal CodeMirror adds about 92 KB gzipped (97 KB with a language hook) to the roughly 322 KB of the wiki's editing bundle. This is accepted for syntax highlighting and a proper undo history.
-| The theme of the editor is written in the tokens of `@telaion/ui` only, so it follows the light and dark switch.
-| Tab leaves the editor for the next control; it does not insert indentation. A keyboard user must not be trapped. Indent with Mod-] and Mod-[.
-| A save writes the text as it was when it started. Text typed while the save runs stays unsaved.
-| A conflict never overwrites. The edit is kept, the session reports the conflict, and resolving it is the job of the application or of `@telaion/tomet`.

==[ Two kinds of conflict ]

The word means two things, and Telaion keeps them apart.

-| **The stored version moved.** A save finds that the document changed since the edit began. The `DocumentAdapter` answers `SaveResult.conflict`, the `DocumentSession` keeps the edit and sets `session.conflict`, and the `Editor` says so. Nothing was written.
-| **The text holds `@conflict` markers.** A three-way merge could not combine two edits, so it kept both inside the document and saved it. This is part of the text. `ConflictResolver` in `@telaion/tomet` lists the markers through `TometServices.listConflicts` and replaces one at a time through `resolveConflict`.

`ConflictResolver` takes the document text and the services, and gives the text back through `onResolve`. The list may arrive late and is shown with a spinner the first time; an answer for text that has since changed is dropped; while the engine resolves one conflict, the buttons wait.

==[ Theme ]

The most expensive failure so far was look-and-feel that is regenerated with every rewrite, and hard to steer. The answer is a small fixed vocabulary, decided once by a person.

-| Visual values are CSS custom properties with the prefix `--tln-`. Components reference tokens only.
-| The token set is small and fixed. No shadows, no gradients, one radius. A modal is separated by a border and a scrim, not by a shadow.
-| One default value set ships with `@telaion/ui` as `tokens.css`. An application overrides token values; it does not restyle components.
-| Plain CSS. No utility framework inside the shared packages, so that the same CSS can be embedded in the wiki's Rust assets unchanged.

===[ Decided ]

-| Palette: Stone neutrals and a Sky accent.
-| Light and dark live in one declaration through `light-dark()`. The default follows the operating system; `data-theme="light"` or `data-theme="dark"` on any element overrides it for that subtree.
-| Text pairs meet WCAG AA (4.5:1). The border of a control uses `--tln-border-strong` (3:1); `--tln-border` is for dividers only.
-| The tokens: surface (`bg`, `bg-subtle`, `bg-sunken`), text (`fg`, `fg-muted`), lines (`border`, `border-strong`), accent (`accent`, `accent-hover`, `accent-fg`, `accent-soft`), state (`ok`, `warn`, `danger` and their `-soft` tints, and `danger-fg` for text on a danger fill), `scrim`, fonts (`font-sans`, `font-mono`), text sizes (`text-xs`, `text-sm`, `text-md`), `leading`, `space`, `radius`.
-| `light-dark()` and `color-mix()` require a 2024-or-later browser engine. This is accepted.

==[ Distribution ]

-| The packages are published to npm under the `@telaion` scope. This is a requirement, not a preference: `@telaion/editor` depends on `@telaion/ui`, and a workspace dependency cannot be resolved when one package is installed from a subdirectory of a git repository. The wiki also builds with Deno through `npm:` specifiers, which have no way to take a git subdirectory.
-| They are Svelte library packages, built with `svelte-package` into `dist/` (TypeScript removed, declarations added). Each application bundles them with its own bundler (Vite).
-| Publication comes before the first migration of an application. Until then the API is exercised inside this repository, in the playground, as the rebuilt New Page dialog does.
-| Versions stay at 0.x while the API settles. An application depends on a caret range of one minor version and takes new ones deliberately.
-| While a consumer is tried against unreleased Telaion, link it locally. A committed manifest or lockfile never contains a local path.

===[ How a release is made ]

-| `just build` turns each package into its `dist/` with `svelte-package`: TypeScript removed, declarations added. In the repository a package's `exports` point at its source, so the playground needs no build; `publishConfig.exports` points at `dist/` and replaces them in the published package.
-| Relative imports are written with a `.js` extension, even for TypeScript files. A `.ts` extension survives into the build, where no such file exists.
-| Publish with `pnpm publish`, never `npm publish`: only pnpm turns `workspace:^` into a real version.
-| The three packages are versioned together.
-| Before publishing, pack the three packages, install the tarballs in a scratch application, and check that it type-checks against the published declarations and builds with only the used icons.
-| The first release is made by hand from a laptop (`pnpm -r publish --access public`, with the one-time password). npm's trusted publishing can be set up for a package only after the package exists. From the second release on, CI publishes with provenance.

==[ Out of scope ]

The following stay in the applications.
-| The application shell: file tree, workspace and vault management, tabs, settings.
-| Authentication and anything that talks to a host (Tauri `invoke`, the GitHub API).
-| Rust code. Metadata handling that needs the Tomet AST belongs to the Tomet crates, not to Telaion.

